# Q4 scheduler integration outline — merge into kernel/proc.c, do not paste
# this file as a replacement for proc.c.
#
# Requirements:
# 1. Priority values are 1..20; lower number means higher priority.
# 2. Choose a RUNNABLE process with the lowest numeric priority.
# 3. Rotate ties fairly (round-robin among equal-priority processes).
# 4. Age only RUNNABLE processes that were not selected. Once wait_ticks > 30,
#    improve priority by decrementing it, never below 1, and reset wait_ticks.
# 5. Reset wait_ticks for the process that is selected.
#
# Implementation guidance:
# - The stock xv6 scheduler typically scans proc[] and switches immediately
#   for each RUNNABLE process. Replace that selection policy carefully: first
#   identify the best priority among RUNNABLE processes, then select one tied
#   process using a rotating starting index/cursor.
# - Keep all reads/writes of process state and scheduling fields under the
#   appropriate p->lock. Avoid acquiring the same process lock twice.
# - The stock scheduler's lock handoff across swtch() is intentional; preserve it.
# - Aging all RUNNABLE processes must be synchronized. Do not age the RUNNING
#   process, and do not increment wait_ticks for sleeping/zombie processes.
# - A global cursor may be declared static in proc.c if your kernel version
#   supports the desired tie rotation; protect it if it can be accessed by
#   multiple CPUs.
#
# Add set_priority(pid, priority) and get_priority(pid) handlers (see
# sysproc_additions.c) and syscall wiring. Validate priority in [1,20].
